{
  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Pledges a smooth Brexit outside the Single Market/Customs Union; +£8bn NHS funding and major mental health commitments; social care reform with £100,000 asset floor; build 1.5m homes by 2022; energy safeguard tariff cap to cut bills; reduce net migration to the tens of thousands; balanced budget plan and industrial strategy for jobs and growth; school funding uplift and technical education; transport upgrades (HS2, Northern Powerhouse Rail, Heathrow). TEXT_C: Maximalist Brexit tests (no divorce bill, full legal supremacy, exit SM/CU by end‑2019); net migration to ~zero and 5‑year pause on low‑skilled migration; +£11bn for NHS/social care; remove VAT/levies from energy and hot food; scrap BBC licence fee; grammar school in every town; scrap HS2 and Heathrow expansion; repeal Climate Change Act; major constitutional changes (PR, abolish Lords, English Parliament).",
  "reasoning": "The British electorate in 2017 prioritised Brexit alongside the NHS, the economy/cost of living, housing, immigration and social care, and TEXT_E offers broad, mainstream commitments across all these areas (notably NHS funding, a large housing target, an energy price cap, and social care reform) in addition to a negotiated Brexit. TEXT_C, while strong on Brexit/immigration and some cost‑of‑living/NHS pledges, places greater emphasis on hardline or niche positions and constitutional reforms that were less central to most voters’ stated priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
